  "reasoning": "The claim that humans only use 10% of their brain capacity is a well-known neuromyth. Modern neuroimaging techniques, such as functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and positron emission tomography (PET), show that virtually all regions of the brain are active and have designated functions. Even during sleep, all areas of the brain show some level of activity. Furthermore, evolutionary neuroscience notes that the brain consumes roughly 20% of the body's energy despite accounting for only 2% of its weight, making a 90% dormant organ an evolutionary impossibility. Scientific authorities, including Scientific American and neuroscientific organizations worldwide, have repeatedly debunked this claim.",
